<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Real life: interpreting f and its derivative in context (INNODEMS)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Let \( {@fs@}({@vs@}) \) be {@fdesc@}, where \( {@vs@} \) is the number of {@xpl@} {@xafter@}.</p>
<p>You are told that</p>
\[ {@fs@}({@bb@}) = {@fb@}, \qquad {@fs@}'({@bb@}) = {@dfb@}, \qquad {@fs@}'({@cc2@}) = 0. \]
<p>(a) What are the units of \( {@fs@}'({@vs@}) \)?</p>
<div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) Which statement correctly interprets \( {@fs@}'({@bb@}) = {@dfb@} \)?</p>
<div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>
<p>(c) Which statement correctly interprets \( {@fs@}'({@cc2@}) = 0 \)?</p>
<div>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The derivative \( {@fs@}'({@vs@}) \) is the <strong>instantaneous rate of change</strong> of {@qshort@}.</p>
<p><strong>(a)</strong> Its units are always \( \dfrac{\text{units of the output}}{\text{unit of the input}} \): here, <strong>{@fpl@} per {@xsg@}</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>(b)</strong> \( {@fs@}'({@bb@}) = {@dfb@} \) is {@if dfb > 0 then "positive" else "negative"@}, so at \( {@vs@} = {@bb@} \) {@qshort@} is <strong>{@if dfb > 0 then "increasing" else "decreasing"@}</strong> at a rate of {@abs(dfb)@} {@fpl@} per {@xsg@}. The value \( {@fs@}({@bb@}) = {@fb@} \) is a different fact: it tells you how much there is at that instant, not how fast it is changing.</p>
<p><strong>(c)</strong> \( {@fs@}'({@cc2@}) = 0 \) means that at \( {@vs@} = {@cc2@} \) {@qshort@} is momentarily neither increasing nor decreasing: the tangent to the graph is horizontal there. It does <em>not</em> mean that the quantity itself is zero.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>3</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
